# TemplateSlots Call Walkthrough

In `src/project_variables.rs`, start at `TemplateSlots` to read the declarations for Template Variables. The first four objects located are, in order: on line 11, `TemplateSlots`; on line 18, `VarInfo`; on line 24, `StringEntry`; and on line 31, `ConversionError`.

```rust
pub struct TemplateSlots {
pub enum VarInfo {
pub struct StringEntry {
pub enum ConversionError {
```

These declarations are grouped together, so you can open them in sequence when reading through variable assembly. `ConversionError` is the entry point in this section for checking conversion-failure branches.

Further down in the file, `SupportedVarValue`, `SupportedVarType`, and `RESERVED_NAMES` are also marked. TODO: fill in the actual enum branches and confirm where the reserved-name check enters variable assembly.

# Git Shared Entry Point Walkthrough

Note `src/git/utils.rs` as one starting point for reading the shared Git-related code. On the first pass, the file header brought remote objects, identity paths, directory copying, and output macros together in one place; that led to the name "shared entry point" in the notes at the time.

```rust
use crate::git::remote::{GitRemote, GitUrlAndConfig};
use crate::warn;
use crate::{copy_dir_all, info};
use crate::git::identity_path::IdentityPath;
```

When reading this page, start with `GitRemote` and `GitUrlAndConfig` and trace back to `git::remote`, then look at the objects `IdentityPath` provides on the identity-file side. `copy_dir_all` is the directory operation exposed here; `warn` and `info` are left as markers for output locations.

The first draft casually described directory copying as a cleanup step after remote handling. That wording only reflected a reading based on the import order at the time; it still needs to be checked against the usage sites of `utils.rs`.

# Git author identity and test mode decision record

In 0.14 Compatibility Train, Git Config Author Identity Fallback and Template Expansion Test Mode were listed in closely related requirements entries. The decision in the initial draft was to keep both items, without presenting them as a single confirmed generation flow.

At the time, there were two ways to organize this. The first described the flow from author variables through template expansion, then used test mode as a checkpoint for the fallback result; the second kept author identity on the Template Variables side and test mode on the expansion side. The second approach was adopted because there was no run sample on hand that recorded both Git configuration reads and template testing.

The starting point for looking up author identity is noted at `src/template_variables/authors.rs`. The file header shows:

```rust
use anyhow::Result;
use git2::{Config as GitConfig, Repository as GitRepository};
use std::env;
```

This decision did not settle the precedence among Git configuration, repository, and environment reads, nor did it add commands, arguments, or failure semantics for test mode. For follow-up checks on these two items, return to their respective local entry points.

